<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neukamerun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1911–1916</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as New Cameroon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neukamerun was the name of the territories that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1911, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir-crisis">Agadir Crisis</a>, to enlarge the colony of Kamerun in exchange for German recognition of French control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, the lands gave Kamerun access to the Congo and Ubangi rivers. Allied forces occupied them during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and they returned to France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neukamerun.jpg" alt="Neukamerun" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cameroon_border_changes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
